PR #178  overall: potential_risk
Spec: P-extension double-wide instructions encode register pairs via 4-bit pair-index fields — tools mapping these fields directly to x0–x15 miss half the architectural state, creating blind spots in taint analysis for data flowing through odd-numbered registers
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:1342-1410  (macros) MASK_RS1, MASK_RS2, MASK_RD, GET_RS1_NUM, GET_RS2_NUM, GET_RD_NUM
      These field-extraction primitives hardcode 5-bit register fields (MASK_RS1=0xf8000 bits[19:15], MASK_RS2=0x1f00000 bits[24:20], MASK_RD=0xf80 bits[11:7]) and treat the extracted value as a 1:1 architectural register index. P-extension double-wide instructions encode rs1_p/rs2_p/rd_p as 4-bit pair-index fields in different bit positions (e.g. rs1_p at [15:12], rs2_p at [19:16], rd_p at [9:6]), where each index i addresses the contiguous pair [2*i, 2*i+1]. No P-extension-specific mask/shift/macro variants (e.g. MASK_RS1_P, GET_RS1_P_NUM) exist. If a P-extension handler composes with the existing macros, it would extract the wrong bit range and map the result 1:1 to a single even-numbered register, missing the odd register entirely. Binary analysis tools relying on these macros for data-dependency tracking would produce incorrect taint graphs.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_trap.h:213-220  (macros) REG_VAL, GET_RS1, GET_RS2, SET_RD
      GET_RS1/GET_RS2 resolve to single-register reads via REG_VAL(GET_RS1_NUM(insn), regs); SET_RD writes exactly one GPR. These are the choke-point macros that compose field extraction with GPR array indexing. For P-extension pair instructions, this is fundamentally insufficient: rd_p=3 must write both x6 and x7 (indices 6 and 7), but SET_RD would only write a single register at whatever index the (wrong) field extraction produces. rs1_p=2 must read both x4 and x5. No pair-aware accessors (e.g. GET_RS1_PAIR, SET_RD_PAIR) exist. Since all emulation consumer sites (load/store, atomic, CSR, illegal-insn handlers) compose through these macros, the absence of pair-aware variants means every downstream P-extension handler would inherit the 1:1 field-to-register assumption.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:327-330  sbi_trap_handler
      This is the trap dispatch entry point. CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION traps are unconditionally forwarded to sbi_illegal_insn_handler(tcntx). The trap context (including mtval encoding the faulting instruction) is passed through unchanged. No instruction decoding or register access occurs at this site. The routing is architecturally correct: P-extension instructions that trap as illegal on harts without hardware support will arrive here intact and be delivered downstream. No code change is needed at this call site.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:113-175  sbi_illegal_insn_handler
      The opcode dispatch table illegal_insn_table[32] routes by (insn & 0x7c) >> 2 (bits [6:2]). Currently 3 of 32 slots are populated (misc_mem at 3, atomic at 11, system at 28); the remaining 29 slots, including any that P-extension opcodes would occupy, map to truly_illegal_insn. This is currently safe because unrecognised P-extension instructions correctly trap as illegal. However, when a P-extension handler entry is added to this table, the handler function receives the raw instruction word and must perform its own register-field extraction. There is no mechanism enforcing that the handler uses pair-aware accessors rather than the standard 5-bit GET_RS1/GET_RS2/SET_RD macros. The implicit invariant is 'any handler added to this table for P-extension opcodes will use pair-index-aware register accessors', but nothing in the code validates this.
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:880-1041  (macros) INSN_MATCH_*/INSN_MASK_* family, INSN_OPCODE_MASK
      This file defines INSN_MATCH_*/INSN_MASK_* patterns for standard opcode decoding. No P-extension instruction definitions (PSH1ADD.DH, PSH1ADD.DW, PSSH1SADD.DH, PSSH1SADD.DW) exist. When added, each must encode 4-bit (not 5-bit) register field widths in the mask to avoid matching against bits belonging to other fields. The existing INSN_MASK_* patterns (e.g. INSN_MASK_LB=0x707f) use 5-bit field masks as they target the base ISA's register positions. A P-extension INSN_MASK_ pattern that inadvertently uses 5-bit register masks would match instructions with unintended register operand values. The vestigial vxsat CSR number is also absent from the CSR definitions near line 244. The risk is that P-extension match/mask macros are added without corresponding adjustment to the register-field extraction layer, creating a mismatch between decoding and access.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_ldst.c:158-203  sbi_trap_emulate_load / sbi_trap_emulate_store
      sbi_trap_emulate_load at line 160 uses SET_RD(insn, regs, val) to write a single GPR for emulated load results. sbi_trap_emulate_store at line 203 uses GET_RS2(insn, regs) for a single register value. If P-extension double-wide memory instructions with pair-index fields were emulated here, these single-register accesses would be incorrect — a double-wide load to rd_p=3 must write both x6 and x7, but SET_RD would write only one register. The code does have a precedent for handling non-standard register access: vector load/store instructions (IS_VECTOR_LOAD_STORE) are detected and routed to vector-aware emulators at line 147-149, bypassing the single-register path. No equivalent P-extension detection or routing exists. Currently safe because no P-extension memory instructions are handled, but the invariant 'P-extension memory instructions will never reach this code path' is not enforced.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-p-spec/pr-178/

  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_atomic.c:100-121  DEFINE_ATOMIC_FUNCTION macro, atomic_* functions
      The DEFINE_ATOMIC_FUNCTION macro hardcodes 1:1 field-to-register primitives: GET_RS1(insn, regs) for the address (line 104), GET_RS2(insn, regs) for the operand value (line 105), and SET_RD(insn, regs, rd_val) for the result (line 118). This macro is expanded into concrete atomic_* functions that are placed directly into the illegal_insn_table at opcode slot 11 (sbi_illegal_atomic). If P-extension register-pair instructions ever shared or collided with the AMO opcode slot, the dispatch function would receive pair-index register fields and process them through these 1:1 primitives — reading from only half the source pair and writing to only one destination register. The assumption that 'P-extension instructions will never use the AMO opcode slot' is implicit and unenforced.

